course: language
lesson: 45
title: Dead, a mile long and everyone: more everyday hyperbole
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: People use the biggest words they know for everyday feelings: «I'm DEAD», «the queue's a mile long», «EVERYONE's got them», «I'll KILL you», «I'm melting», «I could eat a horse».
explain: Priya rolling on the sofa at a funny video and gasping «I'm dead, I literally can't breathe» is laughing, not dying. Ella's «I nearly had a heart attack!» when the dog barked means «you gave me a fright».
explain: But hyperbole has a twin trap. Because people exaggerate so often, it is easy to stop listening, and some of those words can be true: a person who really cannot breathe, or a grown-up with real chest pain, needs help at once.
explain: So a wise listener does two things: reads the feeling behind the big words, and checks the real evidence — the sign at the ride, the thermometer, a face that is laughing or a face that is going pale.
fact: «I'm dead» and the skull emoji 💀 are young people's way of saying «this is hilarious».
fact: If someone really looks as if they cannot breathe — gasping, lips going blue, not laughing — you get an adult straight away.
fact: «A mile long» is a feeling about a queue; a sign that says «Wait from here: 45 minutes» gives the real number to decide with.
fact: «EVERYONE in my class has got them» usually means a few children, perhaps four out of thirty; the fair question is «who exactly?».
fact: «Touch my straighteners again and I'll KILL you» means «keep your hands off my things, I'm cross»; it is not a real plan, but if someone says things like that while really hurting or scaring you, you tell an adult.
fact: «Strewth» is an old Australian word of surprise; «hotter than the sun» is hyperbole, because the sun is millions of degrees, but 37 degrees is really hot and needs shade, water and a hat.
fact: In Canada a «toque» (say «took») is a woolly hat, and «eh» is often added to the end of a sentence; minus twenty degrees can hurt bare ears and fingers.
fact: «I'm so hungry I could eat a horse» is a fixed saying for «very hungry»; nobody is eating a horse.
fact: «Older than the hills» means very old; hills are millions of years old, while an old telly might be fifteen or twenty years old.
fact: «My feet are about to drop off» after a twelve-hour shift means they ache badly and she is worn out, not that her feet are coming off.
fact: «I nearly had a heart attack» after a fright means your heart was pounding; real chest pain, especially in a grown-up, is a 999 matter and never a joke.
fact: Hyperbole tells you how someone feels; a measurement (a sign, a clock, a thermometer) tells you how big something really is.
exercise: Priya is rolling on the sofa watching a funny video: «I'm DEAD 💀💀». What does she mean? | The video is so funny she can't stop laughing | She feels ill and needs to lie down for a while | The video is scary and upsetting | Her tablet has run out of battery
exercise: What should you do if someone really cannot breathe, with lips going blue and no laughing? | Get an adult to help straight away | Laugh along, it's only a saying | Wait to see if it passes on its own | Show them a funnier video instead
exercise: «Mate the queue's a mile long, we'll be here all day». The sign says «Wait from here: 45 minutes». How long is the wait? | About forty-five minutes | About a mile | The whole day, as he says | Until the park shuts
exercise: Lily says «EVERYONE in my class has got them». What is usually true? | A few children have them | Every child in the school has a pair | Nobody at all has them yet | Only the teacher has a pair
exercise: What is a fair question to ask when someone says «everyone's got one»? | «Who exactly has got one?» | «Why haven't you got one yet?» | «How much were they?» | «Can I have one too?»
exercise: Josh's sister says «Touch my straighteners again and I'll KILL you». What does she mean? | Keep your hands off my things, I'm cross | She is making a real plan to hurt him | She wants him to try them out on his hair | She is joking and doesn't mind at all
exercise: In Perth it is 37 degrees and a man says «it's hotter than the sun». What is true? | It is very hot, but nowhere near as hot as the sun | It really is as hot as the surface of the sun today | It is only a little warm and he is just being silly | It is cooler than the sun, so it is quite safe to play
exercise: What does «strewth» mean in Australia? | An old word of surprise | A very hot wind | A kind of wide sun hat for the beach | A rude word for heat
exercise: In Winnipeg someone says «It's like minus a million out there, wear your toque, eh». What is a toque? | A woolly hat | A thick coat | A pair of snow boots | A warm scarf
exercise: It is minus twenty degrees. What is the wise reading of «minus a million»? | Very cold, so hat and gloves on | Minus a million is the real temperature | It is only talk, so go out without a hat | It is warm, because people always exaggerate
exercise: Grandad smells the roast: «I'm so hungry I could eat a horse». What should you do? | Give him a big plate of dinner | Hide the horses from Grandad | Tell him horses are not food | Give him a tiny snack instead
exercise: «That telly's older than the hills, that is». How old is the telly probably? | Fifteen or twenty years | Millions of years | A few weeks | Older than Grandad himself is
exercise: Mum comes home from a twelve-hour shift: «my feet are about to drop off». What is the kind answer? | A cup of tea and a sit-down | Ring the hospital about her feet | Ask her to go for a run with you | Tell her feet can't fall off, silly
exercise: Ella's dog barks behind her in the dark: «I literally nearly had a heart attack!». What does she mean? | The dog gave her a really big fright | She needs an ambulance right away | She is angry with the dog for barking | She has a pain in her chest today
exercise: Grandad suddenly has a real pain in his chest and looks unwell. What should happen? | Call 999, because real chest pain is an emergency | Ignore it, because «heart attack» is just a saying | Wait until tomorrow and see if it is better | Tell him a joke to make him laugh it off
exercise: What tells you how big something REALLY is? | A measurement, like a sign or a thermometer | The biggest word that someone uses about it | How loudly the person says it to you | How many times they have said it
test: «priya keeps saying she's dead lol, should I call someone??» She is laughing at a video. What is right? | No, she means it's hilarious, but if she really couldn't breathe you'd get an adult | Yes, «I'm dead» and «can't breathe» always mean someone needs an ambulance at once | No, «can't breathe» is always a joke, whatever happens | Yes, because skull emojis mean danger
test: Your friend wants to skip three rides because «the queue's a mile long, we'll be here all day». The sign says 45 minutes. What is the wise move? | Use the sign's real number to decide | Believe him and skip all three rides | Measure the queue in miles first | Join a different, longer queue
test: «Mum literally EVERYONE has them». Mum asks who, and Lily names four kids out of thirty. What has Lily done? | Used hyperbole: «everyone» meant a few children she noticed | Told the exact truth about the whole class | Counted every child in the school | Forgotten how many children are in her class
test: «my sis said she'll kill me if I touch her stuff, so I'm moving out lol» What is the best reply? | She's cross, not planning to hurt you, so just leave her stuff alone | Move out tonight, because she means every word of it and she will do it | Touch her things anyway, because it means nothing | Ask your sister when she plans to do it
test: A cartoon shows a man in Perth melting into a puddle at 37 degrees. What is the real lesson? | 37 degrees doesn't melt you, but it is really hot, so find shade and water | People really can melt like ice when the weather is very hot | Heat is never dangerous, so the man could stay out in the sun all day long without water | The man should go out at midday when the sun is highest
test: «nah it's just talk, minus a million isn't real, I'll go out without my hat» It is minus twenty. What is wrong with this? | The number is made up, but minus twenty is still dangerous for bare ears | Nothing, if the number is made up then the cold is made up too | Hats only help when it is really minus a million degrees | It's the gloves that matter, not the hat
test: A girl hears Grandad say «I could eat a horse» and bursts into tears for the horse. What has she missed? | It is a fixed saying for «very hungry» | Grandad has really bought a horse | Horses are often served at roast dinners | Grandad wants to ride a horse after dinner
test: «how old is that telly then, older than the hills?» What is the best way to find its real age? | Look for a date on the back or ask when he bought it | Find out how old the hills behind the house are | Multiply the age of Grandad by two | Guess, because nobody can ever know
test: ★★ Mum after a long shift: «my feet are about to drop off». Ella after the dog: «I nearly had a heart attack». What do they really show? | Mum is very tired and Ella got a big fright | Mum's feet and Ella's heart both need a doctor | Mum is cross with work and Ella is cross with the dog | Both of them are telling silly jokes for fun
test: ★★★ «I'm dead», «I'm melting» and «I nearly had a heart attack» are hyperbole. In which case should you NOT treat the words as hyperbole? | Gran is pale and holding her chest | Priya is laughing at a cat video | Dad is hot at a summer barbecue | Ella got a fright from the dog
test: ★★★ The ride sign says «Wait from here: 45 minutes». You join at 2:30. Your friend moans «we'll be here all day». About when will you get on? | About a quarter past three | About half past eight at night | About ten to three | About a quarter to three
test: ★★★ Lily says «everyone has them». In her class of 30, 4 children have them. How many children in her class do NOT have them? | 26 | 4 | 30 | 34
exam: Identify the meaning most reasonably attributed to the utterance «I'm dead», delivered with laughter by a child viewing a comical video. | That the material is exceedingly amusing | That the child requires immediate medical attention | That the child is in a state of fear | That the device has ceased to function
exam: A notice adjacent to an amusement ride states «Wait from here: 45 minutes», whereas a visitor asserts that the queue is «a mile long». Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The notice supplies the measured duration; the assertion conveys a feeling | The assertion supplies the precise length of the queue in miles | Both statements are measurements of equal reliability | Neither statement conveys any information whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression «I could eat a horse»? | A fixed saying denoting extreme hunger | A declared intention to consume an animal | A complaint about the quality of a meal | A request for a smaller portion
exam: It is given that a person has employed the words «heart attack» in jest after a fright, whereas an older adult complains of genuine pain in the chest. Identify the appropriate response in respect of the latter. | It constitutes an emergency, and the emergency services are to be summoned | It is to be regarded as a mere figure of speech | It is to be ignored until the following morning | It is to be answered with a jest
